Well it is not sure that a program will run on the next version of an
OS. Or even the same version on newer hardware.

When porting NetHack to AROS (an almost source code compatible
reimplementation of the AmigaOS) the programm couldn't create the
temporary level files because the int variable that holds the free
space of the target file system was overflowing and returning negative
values.

And even in vanilla NetHack this can happen when starting it on an
Amiga (emulated or real) with file system that have more than
LONG_MAX/2 bytes free.

